What is Galactose Epimerase Deficiency?

Galactosemias are A group of inherited enzyme deficiencies which feature elevations of GALACTOSE in the blood. 
Epimerases are enzymes that catalyze inversion of the configuration around an asymmetric carbon in a substrate having more than one center of asymmetry. 

Synonyms
  GALE deficiency
  GALE-D
  Gale deficiency
  UDP-galactose-4-epimerase deficiency
  epimerase deficiency galactosemia
  galactose epimerase deficiency
  galactosemia 3
  galactosemia type 3
  uridine diphosphate galactose-4-epimerase deficiency

types of EDG:

  Generalized. Enzyme activity is profoundly decreased in all tissues tested.
      Manifestations include hypotonia, poor feeding, vomiting, weight loss, jaundice, hepatomegaly, liver dysfunction, aminoaciduria, and cataracts. Longer-term features that may be seen in those with generalized epimerase deficiency include short stature, developmental delay, sensorineural hearing loss, and skeletal anomalies 
      Prompt removal of galactose/lactose from their diet resolves or prevents these acute symptoms 

  Peripheral. Enzyme activity is deficient in red blood cells (RBC) and circulating white blood cells, but normal or near normal in all other tissues.
  
  Intermediate. Enzyme activity is deficient in red blood cells and circulating white blood cells and less than 50% of normal levels in other cells tested.

Reported difficulty differentiating peripheral and intermediate EDG

Diagnosis/testing: 
The diagnosis of epimerase deficiency galactosemia is established in a proband with impaired GALE activity in RBC or other cells and/or biallelic pathogenic variants in GALE identified on molecular genetic testing 

Management: 
Galactose/lactose-restricted diet 

Prevention: 
Restriction of dietary galactose/lactose appears to correct or prevent the common acute signs and symptoms, but not the developmental delay or learning impairment observed in some affected individuals 

Genetic counseling: 
Epimerase deficiency galactosemia is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner. Appropriate testing when conceiving is recommended

